1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,573
On the ninth of June 2047, at twenty to three in the
morning, Dr. Evelyn Marsh sat down

2
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:21,367
in a chair in a laboratory she had run for fifteen
years, put a cannula in her own arm,

3
00:00:21,387 --> 00:00:23,320
and did not tell anybody.

4
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:30,243
She was forty-five. She had been asking Albert Wesker
for the thing in that vial for eighteen

5
00:00:30,263 --> 00:00:36,365
years, and he had said no four times, and the fourth
time — in 2038, on the ice, in the

6
00:00:36,385 --> 00:00:42,346
wind — he had told her the condition: *a version
that I would be willing to give to a

7
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:43,049
stranger.*

8
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:49,213
She had built it. That is the part that matters and
the part that everyone forgets. The

9
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:55,063
heritable line worked. Eleven primates, four
generations, no tumours, no drift, no cost.

10
00:00:55,083 --> 00:01:00,780
She had run it for four years and it was clean, and
she had written the paper, and the

11
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:05,500
paper was on his desk in Wilmington that
night waiting for the morning.

12
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,980
What she had not done was wait for
him to read it.

13
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:16,245
Log entry. Marsh, E. Zero two forty, ninth June.
Self-administration, four millilitres,

14
00:01:16,265 --> 00:01:18,190
lineage candidate K-nine.

15
00:01:18,469 --> 00:01:23,549
I'm doing this now, and not in the morning, because
in the morning he will say no again,

16
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:28,823
and he will be right to, and I will agree with him,
and then it will be another four years.

17
00:01:29,487 --> 00:01:34,180
(very soft) I've worked for that man for twenty
years and he has never once been wrong

18
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,837
about anything, and I would like to find out what I
am when I am not waiting for him.

19
00:01:39,237 --> 00:01:45,236
The narrator notes that this is the only genuinely
rebellious act in this entire account,

20
00:01:45,256 --> 00:01:51,053
that it was committed by the most loyal human being
in the company, and that its cause

21
00:01:51,073 --> 00:01:52,338
was not resentment.

22
00:01:52,737 --> 00:01:58,268
It was that Albert Wesker had spent twenty years
making a woman believe that his approval

23
00:01:58,288 --> 00:02:04,006
was the only currency in the world, and had then
withheld it, and had done so *on principle*

24
00:02:04,026 --> 00:02:09,370
— and the narrator would like you to consider
whether there is a meaningful difference

25
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:14,859
between that and cruelty, because Owen Carrick spent
the rest of his life deciding there

26
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:15,358
was not.

27
00:02:16,307 --> 00:02:17,647
It took nine days.

28
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:23,898
The narrator will not give you the clinical course.
It is in the literature; Evelyn wrote

29
00:02:23,918 --> 00:02:29,703
it herself afterwards, in the clearest prose of her
life, and it is the most-cited paper

30
00:02:29,723 --> 00:02:31,417
in the history of biology.

31
00:02:31,818 --> 00:02:37,747
What matters here is the eleventh of June, when her
staff — six hundred Absolute personnel

32
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:43,367
who could not disobey her, and who had been
instructed on the ninth to take no action

33
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:49,316
and inform no one — stood in a corridor outside a
sealed room and watched a woman convulse

34
00:02:49,336 --> 00:02:51,168
for four hours on a monitor.

35
00:02:51,568 --> 00:02:53,908
What matters is that one of them broke.

36
00:02:54,307 --> 00:03:01,212
His name was Dr. Anton Reiss. He had been summoned
into existence in 2039. He was, by

37
00:03:01,232 --> 00:03:06,832
every measure the System had ever offered,
incapable of disobedience.

38
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:13,383
At eighteen minutes past four in the morning on the
eleventh of June, Anton Reiss walked

39
00:03:13,403 --> 00:03:19,553
to a terminal and sent a nine-word message to
Wilmington against a standing instruction,

40
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:25,092
and the message said: *Director. Dr. Marsh has
dosed herself. Nine days. Come.*

41
00:03:26,242 --> 00:03:34,110
Anomaly. Absolute personnel, designation Reiss, A.:
instruction refused. This has not

42
00:03:34,131 --> 00:03:40,143
occurred before. I do not have an
explanation. I am recording it.

43
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:46,444
The narrator asks you to hold on to Anton Reiss. He
is four chapters from mattering more

44
00:03:46,464 --> 00:03:48,462
than anyone else in this story.

45
00:03:48,862 --> 00:03:52,312
Wesker was on the ice in fourteen hours.

46
00:03:52,712 --> 00:03:58,657
He did not shout at anybody. He did not remove
Reiss, or the six hundred, or discipline

47
00:03:58,677 --> 00:04:05,033
a single person. He went down to Level 11 and stood at
the observation window for twenty-four

48
00:04:05,053 --> 00:04:10,998
hours, through the worst of it, and did not sit
down, and did not speak except once, at

49
00:04:11,018 --> 00:04:16,002
about the seventh hour, when the monitors did
the thing that monitors do.

50
00:04:16,523 --> 00:04:17,933
(very cold) No.

51
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:21,403
That is all. One word, at a
window, to nobody.

52
00:04:21,802 --> 00:04:23,853
She came out of it on the eighteenth.

53
00:04:24,692 --> 00:04:30,727
The narrator will describe what Dr. Evelyn Marsh was
afterwards as carefully as he can,

54
00:04:30,747 --> 00:04:36,502
because everything that happens in the last twenty
chapters comes out of this room.

55
00:04:36,903 --> 00:04:42,640
She was not a monster. She did not have slit pupils
or claws or any of the things that

56
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:48,464
the eleven levels below her had produced over
fifteen years. She looked like a woman of

57
00:04:48,484 --> 00:04:50,473
forty-five who had slept well.

58
00:04:50,872 --> 00:04:56,851
What she had was this: a cellular biology that would
not age, that could not be infected

59
00:04:56,871 --> 00:05:02,918
by anything, that repaired at a rate her own
instruments could not resolve — and, because

60
00:05:02,938 --> 00:05:08,712
the lineage was heritable and germ-line and she had
built it that way on purpose, the

61
00:05:08,732 --> 00:05:11,302
capacity to pass all of it to a child.

62
00:05:11,702 --> 00:05:17,947
And something else, which the paper does not mention
and which took Wesker about ninety

63
00:05:17,966 --> 00:05:19,242
seconds to notice.

64
00:05:19,762 --> 00:05:20,913
Say something.

65
00:05:21,312 --> 00:05:26,432
I'm sorry. I'm not sorry. Both of those are true and
I've been lying here for two days

66
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:28,943
trying to work out which one to say first.

67
00:05:29,343 --> 00:05:30,813
You disobeyed me.

68
00:05:31,213 --> 00:05:32,283
I disobeyed you.

69
00:05:32,682 --> 00:05:38,538
(very cold) You are the only person in this
organisation who *could*, and I had built

70
00:05:38,558 --> 00:05:42,893
my entire estimate of you on the
assumption that you would not.

71
00:05:43,622 --> 00:05:45,778
Yes. I know. That's why I did it.

72
00:05:46,178 --> 00:05:51,674
And then she looked at him, and the narrator notes
what happened next because six hundred

73
00:05:51,694 --> 00:05:57,499
Absolute personnel were watching on a monitor and four
of them later described it identically.

74
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:03,369
The room went quiet the way a room goes quiet
when Albert Wesker walks into it.

75
00:06:03,769 --> 00:06:06,639
But he was already in the room.
It was not him.

76
00:06:07,709 --> 00:06:09,059
(very soft) Ah.

77
00:06:09,459 --> 00:06:10,389
You feel it too.

78
00:06:10,788 --> 00:06:18,078
I have felt it in four hundred rooms since 2026 and
I have never once been on this side

79
00:06:18,098 --> 00:06:21,438
of it. What is your Presence, Dr. Marsh?

80
00:06:21,838 --> 00:06:26,904
I don't have a panel, Director. I have a room full
of people who cannot disobey me and

81
00:06:26,924 --> 00:06:32,108
who have started, in the last two days, to stand up
when I come in. Nobody told them to.

82
00:06:32,128 --> 00:06:33,408
They weren't built to.

83
00:06:33,809 --> 00:06:39,296
The narrator will not leave Anton Reiss standing in
a corridor, because what happened

84
00:06:39,316 --> 00:06:43,379
to him in the nine days is the whole of
what the Absolute were.

85
00:06:43,778 --> 00:06:49,326
He sent the message at eighteen minutes past four in
the morning on the eleventh. He did

86
00:06:49,346 --> 00:06:55,211
not sleep for the following seven days. On the
fourteenth he was found by a junior technician

87
00:06:55,231 --> 00:07:00,652
sitting on the floor of a service corridor on Level
11, in the dark, having been there

88
00:07:00,672 --> 00:07:06,030
for about four hours, and what he said — the
technician wrote it down the same night,

89
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:07,992
which is why it survives — was:

90
00:07:08,512 --> 00:07:12,943
I was told not to. I know I was told not to. I have
never in my life done a thing I was

91
00:07:12,963 --> 00:07:17,342
told not to do and I did not know that it was
*possible*, and I have been sitting here

92
00:07:17,362 --> 00:07:21,793
since two o'clock trying to work out what else I
have been able to do all this time and

93
00:07:21,813 --> 00:07:22,663
never once tried.

94
00:07:23,062 --> 00:07:29,702
Wesker interviewed him on the nineteenth, alone, for
two hours. The transcript is four

95
00:07:29,722 --> 00:07:30,166
pages.

96
00:07:30,685 --> 00:07:32,036
Why did you send it?

97
00:07:32,435 --> 00:07:35,185
Because she was going to die and
nobody was coming.

98
00:07:35,586 --> 00:07:38,696
(very cold) You were instructed not to.

99
00:07:39,096 --> 00:07:39,905
Yes, sir.

100
00:07:40,305 --> 00:07:41,396
By whom?

101
00:07:42,346 --> 00:07:44,011
(very soft) By Dr. Marsh.

102
00:07:44,850 --> 00:07:51,431
The narrator asks you to notice what Albert Wesker
noticed, in that room, in about four

103
00:07:51,451 --> 00:07:55,300
seconds, and did not say aloud for
forty-one years.

104
00:07:55,701 --> 00:08:01,656
Anton Reiss had not disobeyed the System. He had
disobeyed *Evelyn Marsh*, who was not

105
00:08:01,676 --> 00:08:07,770
his owner, who had been given authority over him by a
man who was — and somewhere in the

106
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:13,676
machinery, in a way nobody has ever been able to
reproduce, that distinction had left

107
00:08:13,696 --> 00:08:15,761
a gap the width of a decision.

108
00:08:16,281 --> 00:08:19,571
Dr. Reiss. Do you know what you are?

109
00:08:19,971 --> 00:08:24,561
Yes, sir. I have known since I was four days
old. It was in my induction file.

110
00:08:24,961 --> 00:08:29,871
(very cold) And has anybody ever asked you
what you would like to do?

111
00:08:30,930 --> 00:08:32,240
(very soft) No, sir.

112
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:33,811
I am asking.

113
00:08:34,841 --> 00:08:43,631
Roster. Reclassification. Doctor Evelyn Marsh.
Human loyalty, ninety-six per cent: no

114
00:08:43,651 --> 00:08:52,442
longer applicable. Designation: Progenitor lineage,
first generation. Classification:

115
00:08:52,462 --> 00:08:52,961
peer.

116
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:56,311
(very cold) Define *peer*.

117
00:08:56,711 --> 00:09:03,181
There are four beings on this planet that I track.
You, and the three you have made without

118
00:09:03,201 --> 00:09:06,177
meaning to. She is the first of the three.

119
00:09:06,576 --> 00:09:10,047
That is not a definition. Define peer.

120
00:09:10,886 --> 00:09:15,360
(flat) A peer is a will I cannot
price against yours.

121
00:09:15,759 --> 00:09:22,175
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker stood in a
corridor on the ice for a long time after

122
00:09:22,195 --> 00:09:27,825
that panel closed, and that what he did next is
the reason this chapter exists.

123
00:09:28,225 --> 00:09:34,182
He did not remove her. He did not restrict her, or
isolate the lineage, or destroy the

124
00:09:34,202 --> 00:09:40,367
material, or do any of the six things that a careful
man does when something he has built

125
00:09:40,387 --> 00:09:42,175
stops being an instrument.

126
00:09:42,575 --> 00:09:43,715
He promoted her.

127
00:09:44,235 --> 00:09:51,587
The Progenitor Initiative is yours in name as well as
in fact. You will report to nobody.

128
00:09:51,607 --> 00:09:59,125
You will publish what you choose and withhold what you
choose. Your budget is not a number.

129
00:09:59,524 --> 00:10:00,528
And in exchange?

130
00:10:00,928 --> 00:10:07,835
In exchange you will tell me the truth about what
you become, as it happens, including

131
00:10:07,855 --> 00:10:14,680
the parts I will not want to hear. And when you
have a second one — and you will, Dr.

132
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:21,768
Marsh, within about five years, because you have
never in twenty years been able to stop

133
00:10:21,788 --> 00:10:26,359
at one of anything — I want to be
told before, not after.

134
00:10:26,759 --> 00:10:28,009
(very soft) That's it?

135
00:10:28,408 --> 00:10:34,626
(very cold) That is *everything*. It is the
arrangement I have with the machine that gave

136
00:10:34,646 --> 00:10:40,723
me all of this, it is the arrangement I have never
once been offered by anyone, and you

137
00:10:40,743 --> 00:10:45,418
are the first person I have ever been in a
position to offer it to.

138
00:10:46,203 --> 00:10:52,679
The narrator will record what she said in reply,
because it is the last thing Evelyn Marsh

139
00:10:52,699 --> 00:10:55,134
ever says to him as a subordinate.

140
00:10:55,653 --> 00:10:57,923
You could have given me this in 2038.

141
00:10:58,323 --> 00:10:59,193
Yes.

142
00:10:59,594 --> 00:11:00,444
Why didn't you?

143
00:11:00,844 --> 00:11:06,901
(very soft) Because in 2038 I would have been giving
it to somebody who worked for me,

144
00:11:06,921 --> 00:11:12,979
and it would have been a gift, and you would have
spent the rest of your life grateful

145
00:11:12,999 --> 00:11:13,473
for it.

146
00:11:13,754 --> 00:11:20,488
You took it. Nobody can take that back off you now,
including me. That is worth a great

147
00:11:20,508 --> 00:11:27,398
deal more than nine years, and I would have said so
at the time if I had had the faintest

148
00:11:27,418 --> 00:11:29,183
idea how to arrange it.

149
00:11:29,583 --> 00:11:31,014
She looked out at the ice.

150
00:11:31,533 --> 00:11:35,198
(very soft) That's the closest you've
ever come to an apology.

151
00:11:35,599 --> 00:11:38,729
Yes. Do not wait for another.

152
00:11:39,759 --> 00:11:47,917
Note. Kill count, A. Wesker, this world, twenty
twenty-six to date: zero. Projected on

153
00:11:47,937 --> 00:11:56,761
current trajectory: zero. Revised projection, following
the ninth of June twenty forty-seven:

154
00:11:56,781 --> 00:11:57,616
unstable.

155
00:11:58,206 --> 00:12:05,559
The narrator said two chapters ago that the ninth of
June 2047 was the date on which Albert

156
00:12:05,579 --> 00:12:08,475
Wesker's record stopped being clean.

157
00:12:08,875 --> 00:12:16,066
He did not kill anyone that day. He was in
Wilmington, asleep, four thousand miles away.

158
00:12:16,466 --> 00:12:22,527
What happened on the ninth of June 2047 is that a
woman he had made proved that the things

159
00:12:22,547 --> 00:12:28,271
he had made could choose — and everything he does
for the next forty-four years is an

160
00:12:28,291 --> 00:12:31,786
answer to that, and not all of the
answers are good.
